Picture 2007. A photo printing website wants to print the pictures you keep on a photo-sharing site. The easy way: ask for your photo-site username and password, log in as you, and download. The OAuth 2.0 specification (RFC 6749) opens with exactly this example and lists what goes wrong:

- the printing site stores your password, usually in clear text, for later;
- it gets *everything* your account can do, not just reading photos, for as long as it likes;
- you cannot cut off just this one site without changing your password for all of them;
- if the printing site is breached, your password and all your data go with it.

